{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"954e5887d62b520d1ffbe03d0fd78bf07a4bbcc3749723865012282a79ff0b2c","slug":"book.image-annotation-cv","location":"/book/Image_Annotation_CV.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"2.1 Image Annotation CV","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Ada Carter & Katie Bigham","given":"Ada Carter & Katie","family":"Bigham"},"name":"Ada Carter & Katie Bigham","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"github":"https://github.com/ada-carter/cv","copyright":"2025","numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"source_url":"https://github.com/ada-carter/cv/blob/main/book/Image_Annotation_CV.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/ada-carter/cv/edit/main/book/Image_Annotation_CV.md","thumbnail":"/build/poralia-d801f8e295a2339d4e3a62d3c7ffe416.png","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"Image_Annotation_CV.md","url":"/build/Image_Annotation_CV-9426df588ae8cc09bd097ab82c71b2e3.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"thematicBreak","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"TZ1COVMZkR"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Classes and Annotation","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"e4fzOOUnmr"}],"identifier":"classes-and-annotation","label":"Classes and Annotation","html_id":"classes-and-annotation","implicit":true,"key":"wvARt0C882"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When training models using imagery data, it is crucial to provide the algorithm with properly ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"jvrsj2GW8W"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"annotated","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"GqhcSZmKRK"}],"key":"wNwzxLNDr4"},{"type":"text","value":" training data. This allows the model to learn what to look for and where to find it within an image. Imagine, for a moment, that you are a computer tasked with identifying every rockfish in an image, but the image is completely unannotated, and you have no prior knowledge or context of what a rockfish looks like. You would likely have a (very) hard time piecing together what that request even means. In the context of computer vision, image annotation serves as a vital guide, providing the labels and boundaries necessary for the model to understand its task. Without annotations, the model would be left guessing, unable to differentiate between objects or recognize key features.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"LbYp7g6yUS"}],"key":"IQRtK4P4hL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A critical part of annotation is defining classes. ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"mYJOnj5M9E"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In computer vision, a class refers to a category or label that is assigned to a specific object or feature within an image.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"xurNX7D54H"}],"key":"xVRpQRMFbr"},{"type":"text","value":" Classes are essential for helping the model understand what it’s looking at. Without clear class definitions, the model wouldn’t know how to distinguish between objects like a fish and a rock or identify whether a certain object should be labeled as pollution or marine life. Classes can be very specific, such as labeling an organism down to the species level, like “rockfish,” or more generalized, such as labeling a broader group like “fish” or “jelly.”","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"vhTey8g9Tf"}],"key":"MVgJaqPZKc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the context of marine imagery, classes can vary widely. Sometimes, annotations are done at a species level for detailed ecological studies, while other times, they may simply label a broader morphotype like “fish” or “jelly.” Classes are not limited to biological organisms; they can also represent other features such as geological formations (“ice,” “cobble,” “vent”) or indicators of human impact, such as “plastic debris” or “fishing gear.” The way you define classes has a significant effect on how the model learns to interpret the data. For instance, a class like “plastic” might be used to train a model that detects marine pollution, while a class like “coral” would be used in studies focusing on habitat mapping.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"GNIc5b0X0o"}],"key":"rM3oqoNvGf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Properly defining and annotating these classes allows the computer to understand what it is looking for during training. Without well-defined classes, the model would be unable to make the distinctions necessary to identify objects or features within the images. ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"U6HLgoZiH2"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image annotation refers to the process of labeling images with these classes, which helps the machine learning model recognize objects, patterns, or other relevant features.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"KntXnARIf3"}],"key":"w9zX9B8hvo"},{"type":"text","value":" Depending on the specific task at hand, there are different types of annotations that can be applied, each serving a distinct purpose in guiding the model’s learning process. For this lesson, we will focus on the most common annotation types, as these are foundational for many computer vision tasks and essential for developing accurate and efficient models. Proper annotation not only improves the model’s ability to detect and classify objects but also ensures the quality and reliability of the outputs in real-world applications.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"nYm6bmGfyA"}],"key":"wyTWXF5bBj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are various types of annotations depending on the task, but the most common ones we’ll focus on in this lesson are:","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"xJjM9OqqUo"}],"key":"YddvI2yMFm"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bounding Boxes","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"wucouVbtCH"}],"key":"E63AFpKMsX"}],"key":"MZb55DqVSn"}],"key":"mEy6VY6rtg"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Segmentation","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"SghhJC3nJi"}],"key":"q7XPWucpEI"}],"key":"R8t8FrL3bl"}],"key":"R39PXhcDPA"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keypoint Annotation","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"eqC4hJpSyO"}],"key":"AAZKDRQrRi"}],"key":"JFLh0l8M4K"}],"key":"mA415ZKmDL"}],"key":"Grd6xVJPyt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"These annotation types are essential for different computer vision tasks, such as object detection, segmentation, and pose estimation.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"KLz63mjdta"}],"key":"jh0WgEZ2lk"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bounding Boxes","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"OQvQMcT7zr"}],"identifier":"bounding-boxes","label":"Bounding Boxes","html_id":"bounding-boxes","implicit":true,"key":"RFTWBModx9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bounding boxes are one of the simplest and most widely used types of annotations. They involve drawing a square rectangle around an object of interest in the image and noting the pixel coordinates of each corner as well as the class it is defining.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"bGUmE15bQd"}],"key":"E1a2vxn1PR"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/build/poralia-d801f8e295a2339d4e3a62d3c7ffe416.png","alt":"Multiple Organisms are annotated using bounding boxes. Credit: A.Carter, OOI-NSF","data