, where keypoints represents the head to tail length of a target animal.","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"key":"UTsCRBwlNa"}],"key":"yMUklTN07w"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"PyTorch Annotation Workflow","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"key":"mXgc2ASQrW"}],"identifier":"pytorch-annotation-workflow","label":"PyTorch Annotation Workflow","html_id":"pytorch-annotation-workflow","implicit":true,"key":"KXWC6tjmCc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a typical PyTorch workflow, annotations are stored in a dataset, where each image has a corresponding annotation that contains bounding boxes, masks, or keypoints. The dataset can then be used to train a model, such as ","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"Tvy0Z0M8w4"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faster R-CNN (for object detection)","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"UXzDewZREu"}],"key":"ZTfX0n25vH"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"hCg7Xa3Eqt"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mask R-CNN (for segmentation)","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"pNVus5JPa7"}],"key":"QT16o6socj"},{"type":"text","value":", or ","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"lfmBuOF9Wj"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"HRNet (for pose estimation)","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"SD3CTFil98"}],"key":"b9LpOwaWVs"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"Igm9KOqYnc"}],"key":"ghaJyovBqm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":87,"column":1},"end":{"line":87,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here’s a sample structure for an annotated image in PyTorch:","position":{"start":{"line":87,"column":1},"end":{"line":87,"column":1}},"key":"QttGrMxwLF"}],"key":"ZUfTzHCcmy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":89,"column":1},"end":{"line":89,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'image': image, 'annotations': {'boxes': torch.tensor([[10, 20, 200, 400]]), 'labels': torch.tensor([1]), 'masks': torch.tensor([[[0, 0, 0], [0, 1, 1], [0, 0, 0]]]), 'keypoints': torch.tensor([[[150, 200, 1]]])}","position":{"start":{"line":89,"column":1},"end":{"line":89,"column":1}},"key":"i4n2lrkJk1"}],"key":"To6u9cthjx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"By leveraging these annotations, we can effectively train models for object detection, segmentation, and pose estimation in PyTorch.","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"ELrMQKAgCA"}],"key":"wQT8eM55lt"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quality of Annotations","position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"key":"sCtwQTnsH9"}],"identifier":"quality-of-annotations","label":"Quality of Annotations","html_id":"quality-of-annotations","implicit":true,"key":"ILkR1yUevT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not all annotations are created equal, and poor-quality annotations can significantly impact the performance and reliability of a model. Annotations produced by hasty annotators or automated processes can result in inaccuracies that may mislead the model, leading to faulty predictions or misclassifications. Incomplete, imprecise, or inconsistent labeling can introduce noise into the training data, ultimately reducing the model’s accuracy and effectiveness. These bad annotations can disrupt workflows, causing models to learn incorrect patterns, confuse similar objects, or fail to generalize well to new data. Additionally, poor annotations may lead to an increased likelihood of overfitting, where the model memorizes incorrect relationships rather than learning meaningful patterns. This can be particularly damaging when scaling the model to larger datasets or applying it to real-world scenarios.","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"key":"T9Mwa9BINd"}],"key":"Yr9DS6YP88"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":97,"column":1},"end":{"line":97,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For instance, if bounding boxes are poorly drawn, such as being too tightly cropped (underfitting), too loosely drawn (overfitting), or missing the object entirely, the model may struggle to focus on the object or be misled by irrelevant background. Inconsistent use of class labels across images can also confuse the model, reducing its performance. To better understand how different types of poor annotations can affect model training, the figure below demonstrates three common examples of bad bounding box annotations: overfitting, underfitting, and misidentification.","position":{"start":{"line":97,"column":1},"end":{"line":97,"column":1}},"key":"gUOrwafPgY"}],"key":"Ncgpd9EQPh"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/build/bad_annotations-d705da01536d7060179ffede8cfefaf2.png","alt":"Examples of bad bounding box annotations: overfitting, underfitting, and misidentification. Credit: A.Carter, OOI-NS","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"POTn3JPXPB","urlSource":"images/bad_annotations.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"bad_bbox_example ","identifier":"bad_bbox_example","html_id":"bad-bbox-example","enumerator":"4","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"UQ2PS6eCOI"},{"type":"text","value":"4","key":"cJjDOaRf9K"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"r9X5PYSXKu"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"BDHDv2wYsa"},{"type":"text","value":"Examples of bad bounding box annotations: overfitting, underfitting, and misidentification. Credit: A.Carter, OOI-NS","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"key":"FHqrxQX6Mx"}],"key":"SiDHpQHWCv"}],"key":"F6OPc11Dsv"}],"label":"bad_bbox_example ","identifier":"bad_bbox_example","enumerator":"4","html_id":"bad-bbox-example","key":"x8Wo47qJG6"}],"key":"adPYYtnPKw"}],"key":"Qj4sB4wExl"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Model Interpretability for Marine Computer Vision","url":"/book/model-interpretability-marine","group":"Chapter 1 - Introduction to Marine Imaging"},"next":{"title":"2.2 Image Annotation Man","url":"/book/image-annotation-man","group":"Chapter 2 - Introduction to Computer Vision"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}